\section{Method}

\subsection{Counterfactual Episode Collection}
\label{sec:collection}

Per-step oracle labeling assigns routing labels by comparing single-step outcomes under each computation modality. Under sparse terminal rewards, this approach degenerates: on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, 97.4\% of steps produce identical intermediate scores regardless of modality, yielding no routing signal. We instead perform episode-level counterfactual analysis.

For each task instance, we execute two complete episodes under identical initial conditions (same random seed, same task variation). The \emph{pure-internal} episode follows the ReAct paradigm \cite{react-2023}, where the LLM interleaves chain-of-thought reasoning with environment actions. The \emph{pure-deterministic} episode uses the environment simulator to compute exact state transitions for each action. In both cases the LLM selects which action to take; deterministic computation replaces only the state-transition prediction, not action selection---equivalent to giving the agent access to a perfect world model.

Each episode pair produces a tuple $(s^{\text{int}}, s^{\text{det}}, d^{\text{int}}, d^{\text{det}})$, where $s$ denotes the terminal score and $d \in \{0,1\}$ indicates agent death. We use Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (vLLM) on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, a text-based environment spanning 30 task types with a $-100$ death penalty, with episodes capped at 30 steps. After prescreening, we collect 245 episode pairs across 11 task types (20--40 per type).

\subsection{Death-Aware Labeling}
\label{sec:labeling}

Each counterfactual pair receives an episode-level routing label based on $(s^{\text{int}}, s^{\text{det}}, d^{\text{int}}, d^{\text{det}})$. ScienceWorld scores are integer-valued, so we use exact (in)equality with no threshold. The label is assigned by one of four mutually exclusive cases:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Death asymmetry}: $d^{\text{int}} \neq d^{\text{det}}$ $\rightarrow$ the surviving strategy is preferred.
    \item \textbf{Both dead}: $d^{\text{int}} = d^{\text{det}} = 1$ $\rightarrow$ \texttt{no\_preference}.
    \item \textbf{Score comparison}: both survive and $s^{\text{det}} \neq s^{\text{int}}$ $\rightarrow$ the higher-scoring strategy is preferred.
    \item \textbf{Tied alive}: both survive and $s^{\text{det}} = s^{\text{int}}$ $\rightarrow$ \texttt{no\_preference}.
\end{enumerate}

Label distributions are reported in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}.

\subsection{Death Avoidance Factor}
\label{sec:daf}

To quantify the contribution of death avoidance to routing signal, we define the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF):
\begin{equation}
    \text{DAF} = 1 - \frac{\Delta_{\text{nd}}}{\Delta_{\text{full}}}
\end{equation}
where $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is the mean score gap ($s^{\text{det}} - s^{\text{int}}$) across all episodes and $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$ is the same gap restricted to episodes where neither strategy results in death. DAF $= 1$ indicates all routing signal stems from death avoidance; DAF $= 0$ indicates all signal stems from non-death performance differences. When $\Delta_{\text{full}} = 0$, DAF is undefined, as no routing signal exists to decompose; we report DAF only for task types where $|\Delta_{\text{full}}| > 0$.

Results using DAF are reported in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}.

\subsection{Rule-Based Baseline}
\label{sec:rule}

We test keyword-based routing that selects deterministic computation when the action contains science-related verbs. Three keyword lists of decreasing breadth produce match rates of 85.8\% (v1, degenerating to always-deterministic), 4.9\% (v2), and 2.8\% (v3, both degenerating to always-internal). ScienceWorld actions use generic interaction verbs (\texttt{pick up}, \texttt{move}) rather than science-specific terms; no keyword granularity bridges the gap between near-total and negligible match rates.

